1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,686
Welcome to Class 11: European and German data protection for biomedical research.

2
00:00:05,686 --> 00:00:09,951
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:09,951 --> 00:00:16,585
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:16,585 --> 00:00:18,954
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:18,954 --> 00:00:27,102
After this class, you should be able to distinguish direct identifiers from sensitive biomedical research data,

6
00:00:27,102 --> 00:00:35,250
understand why pseudonymised data remain personal data, and recognise when RCC is an appropriate controlled research

7
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:35,759
environment.

8
00:00:35,759 --> 00:00:38,814
This class is practical RCC training.

9
00:00:38,814 --> 00:00:44,925
It does not replace the project's legal basis, ethics approval, consent documents,

10
00:00:44,925 --> 00:00:51,036
data-management plan, institutional policy, information-security review, or advice from the data-protection officer.

11
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:56,930
RCC may process approved biomedical research data inside its controlled research enclave,

12
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:02,824
but direct identifiers and re-identification keys must not be stored in RCC.

13
00:01:02,824 --> 00:01:09,209
Examples of direct identifiers that must not be uploaded include: names or initials;

14
00:01:09,209 --> 00:01:15,103
postal or email addresses; telephone or fax numbers; patient, case, insurance, personnel,

15
00:01:15,103 --> 00:01:21,488
or hospital record numbers; identifying free text, letters, referrals, consent forms, or screenshots;

16
00:01:21,488 --> 00:01:29,346
account credentials, access tokens, or authentication data; a lookup table that connects study codes to named

17
00:01:29,346 --> 00:01:29,838
individuals.

18
00:01:29,838 --> 00:01:36,223
The absence of these fields does not automatically make the remaining data anonymous.

19
00:01:36,223 --> 00:01:42,608
The research project must still have an appropriate legal basis and governance decision.

20
00:01:42,608 --> 00:01:49,987
The EU General Data Protection Regulation treats genetic data, biometric data used for unique identification,

21
00:01:49,987 --> 00:01:54,907
and data concerning health as special categories of personal data.

22
00:01:54,907 --> 00:02:01,302
Their processing requires an applicable legal basis and safeguards appropriate to the risk.

23
00:02:01,302 --> 00:02:07,205
RCC contributes technical safeguards through controlled access, named user accounts, project-group permissions,

24
00:02:07,205 --> 00:02:11,141
auditability, restricted services, and a managed research-computing environment.

25
00:02:11,141 --> 00:02:19,012
Technical safeguards do not create the legal basis; they help implement an approved research project safely.

26
00:02:19,012 --> 00:02:22,456
Biomedical data can become identifying through context.

27
00:02:22,456 --> 00:02:30,327
Examples include: a very rare diagnosis in a small cohort; exact treatment or sampling dates linked

28
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:34,263
to known clinical events; family relationships or pedigrees.

29
00:02:34,263 --> 00:02:39,971
### Suitable under the normal project workflow Examples include: synthetic teaching data;

30
00:02:39,971 --> 00:02:46,156
public reference genomes and public datasets used under their licence; non-human experimental data;

31
00:02:46,156 --> 00:02:52,816
aggregate statistics and approved derived results; approved individual-level biomedical research data without direct identifiers;

32
00:02:52,816 --> 00:02:59,952
approved genomic data; approved X-ray, CT, MRI, and other medical images after required metadata handling;

33
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:07,564
pseudonymised research data when the project governance explicitly covers RCC and the re-identification key is held

34
00:03:07,564 --> 00:03:08,039
separately.

35
00:03:08,039 --> 00:03:15,651
### Requires a project-level review before transfer Pause and confirm the approved workflow when the data

36
00:03:15,651 --> 00:03:22,311
include: a new data source not listed in the project documentation; detailed free text;

37
00:03:22,311 --> 00:03:24,214
a rare cohort or.

38
00:03:24,214 --> 00:03:29,519
Individual-level human genomic data can be scientifically valuable and computationally demanding.

39
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:37,235
RCC can support such research when the project has established the permitted purpose and access model.

40
00:03:37,235 --> 00:03:44,470
Good practice includes: keep subject identity and the re-identification key in a separate approved system;

41
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:52,186
use study codes rather than names or clinical identifiers; grant access only to named project members;

42
00:03:52,186 --> 00:03:59,902
document the source, purpose, permitted analyses, retention period, and deletion process; avoid unnecessary exports and local

43
00:03:59,902 --> 00:04:06,172
convenience copies; release results only through the project's approved disclosure process; consider relatives,

44
00:04:06,172 --> 00:04:10,512
rare variants, and external linkage when evaluating disclosure risk.

45
00:04:10,512 --> 00:04:14,853
The objective is controlled research use—not a claim that.

46
00:04:14,853 --> 00:04:20,441
Medical images can be used in RCC when covered by the project governance.

47
00:04:20,441 --> 00:04:27,319
The main practical checks are: remove or transform direct identifiers in DICOM headers as required by

48
00:04:27,319 --> 00:04:32,477
the approved export process; check for burned-in names, numbers, dates, or annotations;

49
00:04:32,477 --> 00:04:39,355
keep the linkage key outside RCC; preserve scientifically necessary image information; restrict access to the project

50
00:04:39,355 --> 00:04:45,803
group; do not place images or derived visualisations on public web pages without disclosure review.

51
00:04:45,803 --> 00:04:52,251
An image may contain anatomy that could contribute to indirect identification, especially in head imaging,

52
00:04:52,251 --> 00:04:58,699
but that does not make every image a direct identifier or make research processing impossible.

53
00:04:58,699 --> 00:05:02,794
Use only the data needed for the approved purpose.

54
00:05:02,794 --> 00:05:08,253
In practice: select required columns instead of copying a complete clinical export;

55
00:05:08,253 --> 00:05:13,713
avoid detailed dates or locations when the analysis does not need them;

56
00:05:13,713 --> 00:05:19,173
transfer derived results rather than source data when this preserves the science;

57
00:05:19,173 --> 00:05:25,088
keep one authoritative project copy rather than personal duplicates; define retention and deletion;

58
00:05:25,088 --> 00:05:31,457
restrict access to named project members; avoid publishing small-cell or rare-combination outputs without review.

59
00:05:31,457 --> 00:05:38,737
Data minimisation often improves transfer speed, storage use, reproducibility, and analysis clarity as well as privacy.

60
00:05:38,737 --> 00:05:47,359
Use these official sources for orientation: EU General Data Protection Regulation (official EUR-Lex text) — especially

61
00:05:47,359 --> 00:05:52,208
Articles 4, 5, 9, 25, 32, 35, and 89.

62
00:05:52,208 --> 00:06:00,291
German Federal Data Protection Act, Section 22 — safeguards for special categories of personal data.

63
00:06:00,291 --> 00:06:08,913
German Health Data Use Act — use of health data for public-interest research and healthcare development.

64
00:06:08,913 --> 00:06:17,535
German Health Data Use Act, Section 7 — purpose limitation, confidentiality, and prohibition of re-identification for

65
00:06:17,535 --> 00:06:20,768
data made available under the Act.

66
00:06:20,768 --> 00:06:27,773
German Criminal Code, Section 203 — protection of private secrets and professional confidentiality.

67
00:06:27,773 --> 00:06:29,929
EDPB: anonymisation and pseudonymisation.

68
00:06:29,929 --> 00:06:32,084
Universitätsklinikum Essen data-protection information.

69
00:06:32,084 --> 00:06:34,240
These links support training.

70
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,318
They do.

71
00:06:35,318 --> 00:06:41,400
This class does not use an automated research-file scanner or a machine-generated legal decision.

72
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:44,876
Completion is based on understanding and project governance.

73
00:06:44,876 --> 00:06:51,827
Before moving data into RCC, you should be able to answer: What is the approved research

74
00:06:51,827 --> 00:06:52,261
purpose?

75
00:06:52,261 --> 00:06:55,737
Which data categories are required for that purpose?

76
00:06:55,737 --> 00:07:00,516
Have direct identifiers and the re-identification key been excluded from RCC?

77
00:07:00,516 --> 00:07:06,598
Does the project documentation cover genomic data, images, pseudonymised records, or linkages being used?

78
00:07:06,598 --> 00:07:13,115
Who may access the data, and how will access be removed when no longer needed?

79
00:07:13,115 --> 00:07:17,894
Where will authoritative data, intermediate files, and final results be stored?

80
00:07:17,894 --> 00:07:20,935
What is the retention and deletion plan?.

